{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/0","view":"value","value":{"id":"R001","inputs":["H002","H004","H005","M001"],"output":"N001","definitions":["D001","D002","D003","D004","D005","D006","D008"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","exists_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V","scope":"R001/argument","start":"S001","end":"S004","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix an arbitrary x∈V.","objects":["V"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"r","type":"positive real","scope":"R001/argument","start":"S001","end":"S004","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix an arbitrary r>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"a","type":"real scalar","scope":"R001/argument","start":"S002","end":"S004","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose a from H005 at (x,r), so u_tt(x,r)−Δu(x,r)=a u(x,r).","objects":["u","u_tt","Δu","x","r"],"from_inputs":["H005"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"j","type":"integer with 1≤j≤n","scope":"R001/argument","start":"S003","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"For the spatial identities fix any spatial index j.","objects":["n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]}],"argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix x∈V and r>0. H002 supplies continuous derivatives of every order near (x,r). H004 implies u·u=1. For the time-curve applications, extend u and its positive-time derivatives by zero at nonpositive times; these extensions agree with the original functions near every r>0. Apply M001 to the square of each real component of this total time curve and add the m derivatives. Differentiating this constant identity gives 2sum_{k=1}^m u_k (u_t)_k=2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. Since the time and point were arbitrary this identity holds on the entire open domain.","uses":["H002","H004","M001"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","Δu","n","m","V","W","x","r"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"For every y∈V and ρ>0, u(y,ρ)·u_t(y,ρ)=0.","binders":[{"symbol":"y","type":"point of V"},{"symbol":"ρ","type":"positive real"}],"objects":["u","u_t","V"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Choose the pointwise scalar a using H005. Dot u_tt−Δu=a u with u_t. By F001 and symmetry of the real dot product, u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=a(u_t·u)=0. This uses only the value of a at the chosen point, with no differentiation or regularity of a.","uses":["H005"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","Δu","n","m","V","W","x","r","a"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"u_t(x,r)·(u_tt(x,r)−Δu(x,r))=0.","binders":[],"objects":["u_t","u_tt","Δu","x","r"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Differentiate the identity in F001 in time, using M001 on each component product and H002 to supply both derivatives: |u_t|^2+u·u_tt=0. For a spatial index j, differentiating u·u=1 in x_j by M001 first gives u·u_j=0 at every point. Differentiate that identity once more in x_j, again by M001, to obtain |u_j|^2+u·u_jj=0. These finite component sums establish u·u_tt=−|u_t|^2 and u·u_jj=−|u_j|^2 for every j.","uses":["H002","H004","M001"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","Δu","n","m","V","W","x","r","j"],"produces":[{"id":"F003","statement":"u(x,r)·u_tt(x,r)=−|u_t(x,r)|^2, and for every 1≤i≤n, u(x,r)·u_ii(x,r)=−|u_i(x,r)|^2, where u_i and u_ii are the i-indexed members of the globally defined families u_j and u_jj.","binders":[{"symbol":"i","type":"integer with 1≤i≤n"}],"objects":["u","u_tt","u_t","u_j","u_jj","n","x","r"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S004","text":"Dot the chosen normality equation with u. H004 gives |u|^2=1; F003 and Δu=sum_j u_jj therefore give a=u·(u_tt−Δu)=sum_j|u_j|^2−|u_t|^2. The same calculation applies to any real scalar satisfying that equation, hence gives the claimed universally conditional scalar formula. Substituting it back gives the explicit wave-map equation. F001, F002 and F003 give all remaining clauses. Generalize over x and r; the existentially selected scalar does not escape.","uses":["H004"],"uses_local":["F001","F002","F003"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","Δu","n","m","V","W","x","r","a"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]}],"applications":[{"id":"A001","input":"M001","at":"S001","bindings":"Take 𝕜=Z=R and φ=ψ=the kth component of ρ↦u(x,ρ) for ρ>0, extended by zero for ρ≤0, z=r, for each k=1,...,m.","hypothesis_checks":["The ordinary real field has the required normed field/ring/algebra structures.","H002 supplies derivative (u_t)_k of the time component.","The second factor is the same smooth component and has the same derivative."]},{"id":"A002","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For the time derivative of F001 take φ=the kth component of u(x,·), ψ=the kth component of u_t(x,·), and z=r; each time curve is extended by zero for nonpositive times, and 𝕜=Z=R.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are those of R.","H002 gives derivative (u_t)_k.","H002 gives derivative (u_tt)_k."]},{"id":"A003","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For the first spatial derivative of the unit constraint, set φ=ψ=the kth component of the x_j-coordinate line through (x,r), and z=x_j; 𝕜=Z=R.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are real.","H002 gives the coordinate derivative (u_j)_k.","The identical second factor has that same derivative."]},{"id":"A004","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For the second spatial calculation take φ=the kth component of u and ψ=the kth component of u_j along the x_j-coordinate line, at x_j; 𝕜=Z=R.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are real.","H002 supplies derivative (u_j)_k.","H002 supplies derivative (u_jj)_k."]}]},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/1","view":"value","value":{"id":"R002","inputs":["H001","H002","H006","M003","M009","M010"],"output":"N002","definitions":["D001","D002","D003","D004","D005","D006","D007","D008","D009","D010","D011","D012","D013","D014"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","exists_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"t0","type":"positive real","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix t0>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"J","type":"closed real interval","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"Put J=[t0/2,3t0/2].","objects":["t0"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"C","type":"compact subset of V","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"witness","declaration":"Apply H006 to the positive endpoints t0/2<3t0/2 and choose its compact set C.","objects":["V","t0","J"],"from_inputs":["H006"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"L","type":"real","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S003","end":"S005","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose the bound L supplied by F001.","objects":["C"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":["F001"]},{"symbol":"R","type":"positive real","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S003","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"Set R=max(L,0)+1.","objects":["L"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"Q","type":"subset of V","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S003","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"Set Q=[−R,R]^n.","objects":["R","n","V"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"r","type":"time in int J","scope":"R002/argument","start":"S004","end":"S004","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"For the section argument fix arbitrary r∈int J.","objects":["J"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]}],"argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix t0>0 and put J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. Its endpoints satisfy 0<t0/2<3t0/2, so H006 supplies a compact spatial set C on whose complement u=q for every time in J. Compact sets in Euclidean space are closed; hence (V\\C)×int J is open. At each of its points u agrees with the constant q on a neighborhood. Every positive-order derivative is therefore zero there: difference quotients of a locally constant function are zero, and this applies successively to all orders. H002 gives continuity of all derivatives on the entire open domain.","uses":["H002","H006"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["u","q","n","m","V","W","e","e_t","A","B","T","g","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","t0","J","C"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Apply M009 to the compact set C and the identity map V→V. The identity is continuous, so it gives a real uniform norm bound even when C is empty.","uses":["M009"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["u","q","n","m","V","W","e","e_t","A","B","T","g","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","t0","C"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"There exists a real L with |x|≤L for all x∈C.","binders":[{"symbol":"L","type":"existential real"},{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V"}],"objects":["C","V"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Choose L with the bound of F001 and set R=max(L,0)+1>0 and Q=[−R,R]^n. For x∈C, every coordinate satisfies |x_j|≤|x|≤L<R, so C⊂(−R,R)^n=int Q. H001 gives positive finite dimensions; Q is a compact measurable coordinate box. M010 with all lower endpoints −R and upper endpoints R gives |Q|=∏_{j=1}^n(2R)=(2R)^n<∞.","uses":["H001","M010"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["u","q","n","m","V","W","e","e_t","A","B","T","g","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","t0","J","C","L","R","Q"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"R>0, Q=[−R,R]^n is compact and measurable, C⊂int Q, |Q|=(2R)^n<∞, and all positive-order derivatives of u vanish on (V\\C)×int J.","binders":[],"objects":["R","Q","C","V","n","u","J"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S004","text":"Fix r∈int J. Smoothness in H002 and the finite component formulas defining e,A,B_j,T_j,g_j imply continuity of these functions and of e_t and ∂_{x_j}g_j. The zero derivatives proved in S001 make all of these sections zero outside C. Their nonzero sets have closures contained in the closed compact set C, so each has compact support. Apply M003 to each section with X=V, Z=R and μ=dx: Euclidean Borel open sets are measurable, and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Thus every listed section is integrable. Apply M009 on Q to each continuous scalar section to obtain a finite absolute bound on Q; together with zero values off Q, the usual absolute integral bound is that constant times |Q|. This also proves the supplied bounded-support estimate.","uses":["H002","M003","M009"],"uses_local":["F002"],"objects":["u","q","n","m","V","W","e","e_t","A","B","T","g","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","t0","J","C","L","R","Q","r"],"produces":[{"id":"F003","statement":"For every ρ∈int J, all sections e(·,ρ), e_t(·,ρ), A(·,ρ), B_i(·,ρ), T_i(·,ρ), g_i(·,ρ), ∂_{x_i}g_i(·,ρ), for 1≤i≤n, are continuous, compactly supported in C, integrable, and have an absolute integral bounded by a finite uniform-in-space bound times |Q|.","binders":[{"symbol":"ρ","type":"time in int J"},{"symbol":"i","type":"integer with 1≤i≤n"}],"objects":["e","e_t","A","B","T","g","C","Q","J","n"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S005","text":"Since V\\C is open and contains ∂Q, every section in F003 vanishes on a neighborhood of ∂Q. The time t0 belongs to int J, so e(·,t0) is integrable. Generalize over the arbitrary t0. The same argument supplies the complete existential common-support assertion and all the asserted bounds for each t0; in particular it proves integrability at every positive time.","uses":[],"uses_local":["F002","F003"],"objects":["u","q","n","m","V","W","e","e_t","A","B","T","g","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","t0","J","C","L","R","Q"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]}],"applications":[{"id":"A001","input":"M009","at":"S002","bindings":"Set X=Z=V, K=C and φ=the identity.","hypothesis_checks":["Finite-dimensional Euclidean V is topological and a seminormed additive group.","H006 supplies compact C, chosen in S001.","The identity map is continuous on C."]},{"id":"A002","input":"M010","at":"S003","bindings":"Set I={1,...,n}, a(j)=−R, b(j)=R.","hypothesis_checks":["n is a finite natural-number dimension; H001 gives n≥1."]},{"id":"A003","input":"M003","at":"S004","bindings":"For each r∈int J apply to each of e(·,r), e_t(·,r), A(·,r), B_j(·,r), T_j(·,r), g_j(·,r), ∂_{x_j}g_j(·,r), X=V, Z=R, μ=dx.","hypothesis_checks":["V has its Euclidean Borel structure with measurable opens, R is normed additive, and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets.","H002 and the finite smooth defining expressions give continuity, as derived in S004.","S001 and F002 give vanishing off the closed compact C; its closed subsets, including these support closures, are compact."]},{"id":"A004","input":"M009","at":"S004","bindings":"For each listed section at the fixed r, set X=V, Z=R, K=Q and φ=that section.","hypothesis_checks":["V is topological and R is seminormed additive.","F002 gives compact Q.","The section is continuous by the smooth expressions in H002, as derived in S004."]}]},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/2","view":"value","value":{"id":"R003","inputs":["H002","M001","M002"],"output":"N003","definitions":["D001","D002","D003","D004","D005","D006","D007","D008","D009","D010","D011","D012","D013","D014"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction"],"locals":[{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V","scope":"R003/argument","start":"S001","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix x∈V.","objects":["V"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"r","type":"positive real","scope":"R003/argument","start":"S001","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix r>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"j","type":"integer with 1≤j≤n","scope":"R003/argument","start":"S001","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix 1≤j≤n.","objects":["n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"U","type":"map V×R→W","scope":"R003/argument","start":"S002","end":"S003","kind":"definition","declaration":"Define U(y,ρ)=u(y,ρ) if ρ>0 and U(y,ρ)=0 otherwise.","objects":["u","V","W"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]}],"argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix x∈V, r>0 and a spatial index j. By H002 every derivative entering u_t,u_tt,u_j,u_jj,u_jt is continuous and has derivatives of every order. Their finite sums and component products, in particular e and g_j, are jointly smooth by the usual finite differentiation rules.","uses":["H002"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","e","e_t","g","A","B","T","n","m","V","W","x","r","j"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"For this fixed index j, u_t,u_tt,u_j,u_jj,u_jt and the scalar expressions e,g_j,A,B_j,T_j are jointly smooth on V×(0,∞).","binders":[],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","e","e_t","g","A","B","T","n","m","V","W","j"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Define U(y,ρ)=u(y,ρ) for ρ>0 and zero for ρ≤0. Near (x,r) it agrees with u since r>0. H002 gives that U is C^2 at this point. Apply M002 over R to U at (x,r), with smoothness order 2; the real minimum smoothness is 2. Evaluate the symmetric second derivative on the time direction (0,1) and the jth spatial coordinate direction. Coordinate directional derivatives are the corresponding partial derivatives, so ∂_r u_j(x,r)=∂_{x_j}u_t(x,r)=u_jt(x,r). This works at every positive time and point.","uses":["H002","M002"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","e","e_t","g","A","B","T","n","m","V","W","x","r","j","U"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"For every y∈V, ρ>0 and 1≤i≤n, ∂_ρ u_i(y,ρ)=u_it(y,ρ), where u_it is the i-indexed member of u_jt.","binders":[{"symbol":"y","type":"point of V"},{"symbol":"ρ","type":"positive real"},{"symbol":"i","type":"integer with 1≤i≤n"}],"objects":["u_j","u_jt","V","n"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Extend each time curve below by zero at nonpositive times; this does not change it near the chosen r>0. Apply M001 over R to each squared component of u_t in the time variable and to each squared component of u_j in the time variable. Add the finitely many component derivatives and use F002 to replace ∂_r u_j by u_jt. This yields e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2sum_{i=1}^n u_i·u_it=2A+2sum_{i=1}^n B_i. Apply M001 to each component product in g_j along the x_j-coordinate line: ∂_{x_j}g_j=u_jt·u_j+u_t·u_jj=B_j+T_j. F001 gives continuity of all terms. Generalize over x,r,j to obtain all the asserted identities and regularity.","uses":["M001"],"uses_local":["F001","F002"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","e","e_t","g","A","B","T","n","m","V","W","x","r","j","U"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]}],"applications":[{"id":"A001","input":"M002","at":"S002","bindings":"Set 𝕜=R, X=V×R, Z=W, φ=U, z=(x,r), ℓ=2; evaluate at the time direction and jth spatial direction.","hypothesis_checks":["These finite-dimensional real spaces are normed additive commutative groups and normed real vector spaces.","U agrees with the jointly smooth u on the open positive-time neighborhood of (x,r) by H002 and r>0.","minSmoothness(R,2)=2≤2."]},{"id":"A002","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For the time derivative of |u_t|^2 take 𝕜=Z=R, φ=ψ=each component of u_t(x,·), z=r, extending the component curve by zero for nonpositive times.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are real.","F001 and H002 give its derivative as the corresponding component of u_tt.","The identical second factor has the same derivative."]},{"id":"A003","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For the time derivative of |u_j|^2 take 𝕜=Z=R, φ=ψ=each component of u_j(x,·), z=r, extending the component curve by zero for nonpositive times.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are real.","F001 gives differentiability and F002 identifies the derivative as the corresponding component of u_jt.","The second factor is identical."]},{"id":"A004","input":"M001","at":"S003","bindings":"For ∂_{x_j}g_j take 𝕜=Z=R; φ and ψ are the kth components of u_t and u_j along the jth coordinate line, for k=1,...,m.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are real.","H002 gives the derivative of the first factor as (u_jt)_k.","H002 gives the derivative of the second factor as (u_jj)_k."]}]},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/3","view":"value","value":{"id":"R004","inputs":["N002","N003","M004","M005","M009","M013","M014"],"output":"N004","definitions":["D001","D002","D009","D010","D015"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","exists_elimination","case_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"t0","type":"positive real","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix t0>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"C","type":"compact subset of V","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose C together with R as asserted in N002 at t0.","objects":["V","t0"],"from_inputs":["N002"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"R","type":"positive real","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose R together with C as asserted in N002 at t0.","objects":["V","t0","C"],"from_inputs":["N002"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"J","type":"closed time interval","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"definition","declaration":"J=[t0/2,3t0/2].","objects":["t0"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"Q","type":"spatial box","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"definition","declaration":"Q=[−R,R]^n.","objects":["R","n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"η","type":"positive real","scope":"R004/argument","start":"S001","end":"S006","kind":"definition","declaration…1361 tokens truncated…tegrability of the bound and pointwise, therefore almost-everywhere, convergence. Thus ∫_V Φ(ζ,y)dy tends to ∫_V e_t(y,t0)dy.","uses":["M004"],"uses_local":["F003"],"objects":["V","n","e","e_t","E","t0","Φ","β"],"produces":[{"id":"F004","statement":"lim_{ζ→0,ζ≠0} ∫_V Φ(ζ,y)dy = ∫_V e_t(y,t0)dy.","binders":[{"symbol":"ζ","type":"nonzero real tending to zero"},{"symbol":"y","type":"point of V"}],"objects":["Φ","V","e_t","t0"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S006","text":"For 0<|ζ|<η the two density sections are integrable by N002. M013, applied to e(·,t0+ζ) and −e(·,t0), and M014 with scalars −1 and 1/ζ give ∫_V Φ(ζ,y)dy=(E(t0+ζ)−E(t0))/ζ. Here E is legitimate because N002 proved integrability at every positive time, and t0+ζ>0. Substituting into F004 proves differentiability of the actual energy and E'(t0)=∫_V e_t(y,t0)dy. Generalize over t0>0.","uses":["N002","M013","M014"],"uses_local":["F004"],"objects":["V","n","e","e_t","E","t0","η","Φ","ζ"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]}],"applications":[{"id":"A001","input":"M009","at":"S002","bindings":"Set X=V×R, Z=R, compact set K_source=Q×I and φ=e_t restricted to the positive-time domain, extended arbitrarily outside it.","hypothesis_checks":["The product is topological and R is seminormed additive.","S001 establishes compact Q×I.","N003 supplies continuity on this product, entirely inside positive times."]},{"id":"A002","input":"M005","at":"S003","bindings":"Set Z=R, φ(ρ)=e(y,ρ) for ρ>0, extended by zero otherwise, bound K, and a=min(t0,t0+h), b=max(t0,t0+h), evaluating at b.","hypothesis_checks":["R has its usual real normed structure.","N003 gives smoothness on the interval, which lies in I⊂(0,∞).","F001 bounds |e_t| by L≤K; on the nondegenerate interval its within derivative equals the ordinary derivative e_t."]},{"id":"A003","input":"M004","at":"S005","bindings":"Set X=V, μ=dx, Z=R, I_source=R, l=the punctured neighborhood filter at zero, Φ as in S004, φ(y)=e_t(y,t0), β as in S004.","hypothesis_checks":["V is measurable, dx a measure, R a normed real space; S005 gives an explicit countable basis for l.","F003 gives eventual strong measurability from continuous spatial quotients.","F002 and F003 give domination for every y and all sufficiently small nonzero ζ.","S004 proves β is a finite-integral nonnegative simple function using |Q|<∞ from N002.","N003 and F003 give convergence at every y from scalar differentiability."]},{"id":"A004","input":"M013","at":"S006","bindings":"Set X=V, μ=dx, Z=R, φ=e(·,t0+ζ), ψ=−e(·,t0), for 0<|ζ|<η.","hypothesis_checks":["All structures are the usual Lebesgue/real ones.","N002 gives integrability of e(·,t0+ζ), since t0+ζ>0.","N002 gives integrability of e(·,t0), hence of its negative."]},{"id":"A005","input":"M014","at":"S006","bindings":"Use 𝕜=Z=R, X=V, μ=dx, first c=−1 and φ=e(·,t0), then c=1/ζ and φ=e(·,t0+ζ)−e(·,t0).","hypothesis_checks":["The usual real scalar action has NormSMulClass and commuting real scalar multiplication; V,dx have their declared measurable structures."]}]},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/4","view":"value","value":{"id":"R005","inputs":["N002","N003","M006","M007","M011","M013","M016","M017","M003"],"output":"N005","definitions":["D001","D002","D003","D005","D006","D007","D012","D013","D014"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","exists_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"t0","type":"positive real","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix t0>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"j","type":"integer with 1≤j≤n","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix any spatial index j.","objects":["n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"C","type":"compact subset of V","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose C with R from N002 at t0.","objects":["V","t0"],"from_inputs":["N002"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"R","type":"positive real","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"witness","declaration":"Choose R with C from N002 at t0.","objects":["V","t0","C"],"from_inputs":["N002"],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"Q","type":"spatial box","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S001","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"Q=[−R,R]^n.","objects":["R","n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"Xhat","type":"finite Cartesian product of real lines","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S002","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"Xhat=R^{ {i:1≤i≤n, i≠j} } with product Lebesgue measure; an empty product is the one-point probability space.","objects":["n","j"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"χ","type":"map V→Xhat×R","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S002","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"χ(x)=(the tuple of coordinates x_i with i≠j, x_j). Its inverse inserts its second argument as the jth coordinate.","objects":["V","Xhat","j","n"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"y","type":"point of Xhat","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S003","end":"S003","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix an arbitrary remaining-coordinate tuple y∈Xhat.","objects":["Xhat"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"G","type":"map R→R","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S003","end":"S003","kind":"definition","declaration":"G(z)=g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0).","objects":["g","χ","y","j","t0"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"H","type":"map Xhat×R→R","scope":"R005/argument","start":"S004","end":"S005","kind":"definition","declaration":"H(y,z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0).","objects":["g","χ","j","t0","Xhat"],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]}],"argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix t0>0 and 1≤j≤n. Choose C,R from N002 and put Q=[−R,R]^n. At t0, g_j, its jth derivative and the two summands B_j,T_j are continuous, integrable, and zero outside C⊂int Q. N003 gives smoothness and the pointwise identity ∂_{x_j}g_j=B_j+T_j.","uses":["N002","N003"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j","C","R","Q"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"At t0 the functions g_j, ∂_{x_j}g_j, B_j,T_j are integrable and vanish off C⊂int Q; ∂_{x_j}g_j=B_j+T_j.","binders":[],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j","C","Q"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Define Xhat to be the tuple space of all coordinates except j with product Lebesgue measure, and χ(x)=((x_i)_{i≠j},x_j). Apply M011 with predicate i≠j to split the finite product measure. The remaining block {j} has a unique element; M017 identifies its product measure with ordinary one-dimensional Lebesgue measure by evaluation at j. Composing these explicit measurable equivalences shows that χ is a measure-preserving measurable equivalence from V to Xhat×R. The inverse is coordinate insertion. When n=1, Xhat is the one-point empty product with mass one, so the same identification applies.","uses":["M011","M017"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j","R","Q","Xhat","χ"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"χ:V→Xhat×R and its inverse are measurable, χ is bijective and preserves dx and the product of the Lebesgue measures on Xhat and R.","binders":[],"objects":["χ","V","Xhat"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Fix any y∈Xhat and define G(z)=g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). By N003 this function is smooth and G'(z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). If z=±R, the inserted point lies outside C, since C⊂(−R,R)^n; hence G(−R)=G(R)=0. The derivative is continuous on the finite interval [−R,R], so it is interval integrable there. M006 gives ∫_{−R}^R G'(z)dz=G(R)−G(−R)=0. This is also the integral on the closed interval: its endpoints have Lebesgue measure zero. For |z|>R the derivative vanishes by F001; thus ∫_{z∈ℝ} ∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0) dz=0. This proves the zero inner integral for every y, including when some other coordinate lies outside the box.","uses":["M006","N003"],"uses_local":["F001","F002"],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j","C","R","Q","Xhat","χ","y","G"],"produces":[{"id":"F003","statement":"For every y0∈Xhat, the function z↦∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y0,z),t0) is integrable on the real line and its integral over the real line, equivalently over [−R,R], is zero.","binders":[{"symbol":"y0","type":"point of Xhat"},{"symbol":"z","type":"real"}],"objects":["g","j","χ","t0","R","Xhat","y0","z"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S004","text":"Define H(y,z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). It is continuous by N003 and the explicit coordinate insertion map, and vanishes outside the compact product box corresponding to Q by F001. Thus its support closure is compact. Apply M003 on Xhat×R with its product Lebesgue measure to obtain integrability. Apply M016 with the coordinate equivalence χ and scalar function H: ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat×R}H. Apply M007 to this integrable H. The product Lebesgue factors are sigma-finite, hence s-finite, including the empty factor. F003 makes each inner integral zero. Therefore ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat}0=0. Restricting to Q gives the identical box integral, since its integrand vanishes off Q. This is precisely the coordinate FTC/Fubini integration-by-parts step.","uses":["M016","M007","M003","N003"],"uses_local":["F001","F002","F003"],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j","R","Q","Xhat","χ","H"],"produces":[{"id":"F004","statement":"∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0) dx=0.","binders":[{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V"}],"objects":["V","g","j","t0"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S005","text":"Integrate the identity in F001 and use M013 with its two integrable summands B_j(·,t0),T_j(·,t0). F004 yields 0=∫_V B_j+∫_V T_j. Rearranging gives ∫_V B_j=−∫_V T_j. Unfold the definitions of B and T to get exactly the asserted vector-dot-product formula. Generalize over t0>0 and all spatial indices j.","uses":["M013"],"uses_local":["F001","F004"],"objects":["V","n","g","B","T","u_t","u_j","u_jt","u_jj","t0","j"],"produces":[],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]}],"applications":[{"id":"A001","input":"M011","at":"S002","bindings":"Set I={1,...,n}, each X_i=R with Lebesgue μ_i, and p(i)=(i≠j).","hypothesis_checks":["The coordinate index set is finite and each real line has its Lebesgue measurable structure.","Lebesgue measure on R is sigma-finite (covered by the finite-measure intervals [−k,k]).","Equality of finite coordinate indices is decidable."]},{"id":"A002","input":"M017","at":"S002","bindings":"Use the singleton coordinate type {i:1≤i≤n,i=j}, X_i=R, μ_i=one-dimensional Lebesgue measure; evaluate at j.","hypothesis_checks":["Because 1≤j≤n this finite subtype has exactly one element, j; all spaces and measures are the real Lebesgue ones."]},{"id":"A003","input":"M006","at":"S003","bindings":"Set Z=R, φ=G, a=−R and b=R for each fixed y∈Xhat.","hypothesis_checks":["R is a complete normed real vector space.","N003 and the coordinate insertion formula give smooth G on R, including both endpoints.","G' is continuous on the compact interval, hence interval integrable."]},{"id":"A004","input":"M016","at":"S004","bindings":"Set X=V, Y=Xhat×R, μ=dx, ν=product Lebesgue, Z=R, map χ as in S002 and scalar integrand φ=H.","hypothesis_checks":["All spaces and measures are their finite product Lebesgue structures and R is normed real.","F002 proves the measure-preserving property from M011 and M017.","F002 identifies χ as a measurable equivalence, hence a measurable embedding."]},{"id":"A005","input":"M007","at":"S004","bindings":"Set X=Xhat, Y=R, μ and ν their product/one-dimensional Lebesgue measures, Z=R and φ=H.","hypothesis_checks":["Finite products of Lebesgue measures are sigma-finite, hence s-finite; the empty product has total mass one.","S004 applies M003 to the continuous H with compact support in the product box corresponding to Q; the support bound follows from F001 and the coordinate formula."]},{"id":"A006","input":"M013","at":"S005","bindings":"Set X=V, μ=dx, Z=R, φ=B_j(·,t0) and ψ=T_j(·,t0).","hypothesis_checks":["The spaces are the given real/Lebesgue spaces.","F001 supplies integrability of B_j.","F001 supplies integrability of T_j."]},{"id":"A007","input":"M003","at":"S004","bindings":"Use X=Xhat×R, Z=R, μ=the product Lebesgue measure and φ=H.","hypothesis_checks":["Finite products of Euclidean lines have measurable open sets and Lebesgue measure finite on compact sets, also when Xhat is the empty product.","N003 gives continuity of the derivative of g_j, and composing it with the affine coordinate insertion map gives continuous H.","F001 makes H vanish off the closed bounded product box corresponding to Q, which is compact; its support closure is a closed subset of that box."]}]},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
{"id":"nl0.json","pointer":"/inferences/5","view":"value","value":{"id":"R006","inputs":["N001","N002","N003","N004","N005","M005","M012","M013","M014"],"output":"P","definitions":["D001","D002","D003","D004","D005","D006","D007","D008","D009","D010","D011","D012","D013","D014","D015"],"rules":["direct","forall_introduction","case_elimination"],"locals":[{"symbol":"t0","type":"positive real","scope":"R006/argument","start":"S001","end":"S004","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix t0>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"s","type":"positive real","scope":"R006/argument","start":"S005","end":"S005","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix arbitrary s>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]},{"symbol":"t","type":"positive real","scope":"R006/argument","start":"S005","end":"S005","kind":"arbitrary","declaration":"Fix arbitrary t>0.","objects":[],"from_inputs":[],"from_local":[]}],"argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix t0>0. N002 supplies integrability of e and every derivative product at this time. N004 gives E'(t0)=∫_V e_t(x,t0)dx. N003 gives e_t=2A+2sum_{j=1}^n B_j pointwise.","uses":["N002","N003","N004"],"uses_local":[],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","Δu","e","e_t","E","A","B","T","g","V","n","t0"],"produces":[{"id":"F001","statement":"E'(t0)=∫_V e_t(x,t0)dx, e_t(·,t0)=2A(·,t0)+2sum_{j=1}^n B_j(·,t0), and all these scalar sections and each T_j(·,t0) are integrable.","binders":[{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V"}],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","Δu","e","e_t","E","A","B","T","g","V","n","t0"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S002","text":"Use M013 for the two terms in e_t, M014 to take the factors 2 outside the integrals, and M012 for the finite sum of integrable B_j sections. Then use N005 for every j. This retains the exact calculation E'(t0)=2∫_V u_t·u_tt dx+2sum_j∫_V u_j·u_jt dx=2∫_V u_t·u_tt dx−2sum_j∫_V u_t·u_jj dx. Every integral is finite by F001.","uses":["M012","M013","M014","N005"],"uses_local":["F001"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","Δu","e","e_t","E","A","B","T","g","V","n","t0"],"produces":[{"id":"F002","statement":"E'(t0)=2∫_V A(x,t0)dx−2sum_{j=1}^n∫_V T_j(x,t0)dx.","binders":[{"symbol":"x","type":"point of V"}],"objects":["E","A","T","n","V","t0"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S003","text":"Apply M012 also to the finite family T_j and use M013 and M014 with scalar −1 to combine the difference of integrals. By finite distributivity of the dot product and Δu=sum_j u_jj, A−sum_j T_j=u_t·(u_tt−Δu) pointwise. Thus F002 becomes E'(t0)=2∫_V u_t(x,t0)·(u_tt(x,t0)−Δu(x,t0))dx. N001 makes this integrand zero at every x, so its integral and E'(t0) are zero.","uses":["M012","M013","M014","N001"],"uses_local":["F001","F002"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","Δu","e","e_t","E","A","B","T","g","V","n","t0"],"produces":[{"id":"F003","statement":"E'(t0)=0.","binders":[],"objects":["E","t0"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S004","text":"The time t0 was arbitrary. N002 gives integrability for every positive time, N004 gives differentiability on (0,∞), and F003 gives the zero derivative there. The definitions e=|u_t|^2+sum_j|u_j|^2 and E=∫e preserve the required energy normalization without a factor 1/2.","uses":["N002","N004"],"uses_local":["F003"],"objects":["u","u_t","u_tt","u_j","u_jj","u_jt","Δu","e","e_t","E","A","B","T","g","V","n","t0"],"produces":[{"id":"F004","statement":"For every ρ>0, e(·,ρ) is integrable, E is differentiable at ρ and E'(ρ)=0.","binders":[{"symbol":"ρ","type":"positive real"}],"objects":["e","E"],"assumptions":[]}],"introduces":[],"discharges":[]},{"id":"S005","text":"For completeness fix arbitrary s,t>0. If s<t, restrict E to [s,t]; F004 gives differentiability there and within derivative equal to its ordinary derivative zero. M005 with bound 0 gives |E(t)−E(s)|≤0(t−s)=0, hence E(t)=E(s). If t<s, apply the same argument on [t,s],